RAWMSTAT(1)

# NAME

rawmstat - configurable status producer for rawm

# SYNOPSIS

*rawmstat* [*-p*] [*-c* _path_]

*rawmstat* *-C* [*-c* _path_]

*rawmstat* *-v*

# DESCRIPTION

*rawmstat* executes configured status blocks, composes their textual output,
and publishes the result for *rawm*(1).

With the *rawm-v2* protocol, rawmstat publishes ordered semantic segments to
the *\_RAWM\_STATUS\_V2* property of the X root window. Each segment has a
stable identifier, text, and one of the states *normal*, *warning*, or
*critical*. The property type is *UTF8_STRING*. No process discovery, socket,
root *WM_NAME* convention, color value, or in-band presentation markup is
involved.

Configuration is read at run time.  See *rawmstat.conf*(5) for configuration
precedence, block semantics, update intervals, and realtime update signals.

Unless *-p* is used, rawmstat requires an X display.  It does not start or
supervise *rawm*(1); normally both processes are started by the X session.

# OPTIONS

*-c* _path_, *--config* _path_
	Load built-in defaults followed by exactly _path_.  The normal system and
	user configuration files are not read.

*-C*, *--check-config*
	Load and validate the effective configuration, then exit without opening
	the X display or executing any blocks.

*-p*, *--stdout*
	Write each changed rawm-v2 wire payload to standard output instead of the
	X root property.  This is useful for testing and for text-oriented status
	consumers.  The process continues running and updates according to the same
	interval and signal rules as X mode.

*-v*, *--version*
	Print the version and exit.

*-h*, *--help*
	Print a short usage message and exit.

# RUNTIME MODEL

At startup every configured block is launched once.  Blocks execute
asynchronously, so a slow or hung command does not stop unrelated blocks,
realtime refreshes, X publication, or process shutdown.  A block with a
non-zero *interval* is subsequently requested on its monotonic interval.  A
block with a non-zero *signal* may also be refreshed by sending the
corresponding realtime signal to the rawmstat process.

Only one invocation of a given block runs at a time.  Refresh requests received
while it is running coalesce into one pending rerun.  Every invocation has a
configured timeout and owns its child process group.  Only successful exit
status 0 commits a new sample; failures and timeouts retain the previous
successful value.

Signal handlers only wake the main event loop.  Command execution, child
reaping, configuration lifecycle, and Xlib operations are not performed from
signal context.

Block commands are direct argument vectors executed with *execvp*(3).  Shell
syntax is not interpreted unless a shell is explicitly configured, for
example:

```
command = ("/bin/sh", "-c", "printf '%s' \"$HOME\"");
```

Only the first output line from a block is used. A trailing carriage return is
removed. Plain output becomes a *normal* segment. A line beginning with exactly
*normal<TAB>*, *warning<TAB>*, or *critical<TAB>* selects that semantic state
and removes the state prefix before the configured block prefix is prepended.
Committed segment text must be printable UTF-8 without ASCII control
characters, and the complete *rawm-v2* payload must fit within 4096 bytes.

# SIGNALS

*SIGINT*, *SIGTERM*
	Terminate the main loop cleanly.  Running block process groups are first
	asked to terminate and are forcibly killed after a short bounded grace
	period if necessary.

*SIGHUP*
	Terminate running block process groups, then re-execute rawmstat with its
	original argument vector.  This reloads the selected configuration while
	retaining the rawmstat process ID.

*SIGRTMIN+N*
	Refresh each block whose *signal* setting is _N_.  A signal value of zero
	disables signal-triggered refresh for that block.  Availability of realtime
	signal offsets is validated at startup.

# FILES

_/etc/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	System configuration, when present.

_$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	User configuration when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is set to an absolute path.

_$HOME/.config/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	User configuration fallback.

# EXIT STATUS

*0* on normal termination or successful configuration validation; *>0* on a
configuration, display, setup, or runtime publication error.

# SEE ALSO

*rawm*(1), *rawm.conf*(5), *rawmstat.conf*(5), *libconfig*(5)

